===== 4483 Page 42 | {}
630 P. M  Ft Monroe Va. 6 PM
Feby. 29. 1864
Maj Gen Halleck Dispatch rec'd I will advance
to New Kent C H two thousand
Infy a battery & a thousand cavly
under an Experienced Officer starting on the
Evening of March first to reach New
Kent at 3 Pm Wednesday to aid in
case of disaster to
receive prisoners or to
cover retreat Is this
approved? Benj. F. Butler
Major Genl.


840 AM Mar 4th  Fort Monroe Va Mar 3rd
For Secy of War. The following dispatch received from
my Aid at Ft Magruder who went out 
with my forces to New Kent C
H " Arrived all safe. Genl 
Kil = patrick is here having lost
less than one hundred and fifty men
among whom were Colonels Dahlgren Cook &
Litchfield . Colonels Dahlgren & Cook are 
supposed to be prisoners Litchfield either wounded
or killed . Went within outer line 
of fortifications was engaged until dark with
the Enemy . Colonel Spear will be 
in tomorrow " signed BF. Butler 8 PM

===== 4601 Page 160 | {}
4.30 P. M
Ft Monroe Va May 6th 64
Off City Point 9 pm May fifth via Ft Monroe 130 am
May 6th for Gen Grant Washn ---- we have
seized Wilsons Wharf landing a Brigade (wilds Colored
troops there ---- Fort Powhattan landing two Regt
of same brigade ---- have landed at City Point
Hinks Div of Colored troops remaining"  Brigade & &
battery - Remainder of both Eighteenth and 10th
Army Corps are now being landed at Bermuda
hundreds above the appo - Mattox ---- no opposition
thus far apparently a complete surprise both army
corps left Yorktown during last night ---- Monitors
all over the bar at Harrisons landing and
above City Point the operation of the fleet
have been conducted today with energy and success
---- Genls Smith & Gen Gillmore are pushing the
landing of their men Genl Graham with the
army Gunboats led the advance during the night
capturing the signal stations of the rebels ----
Col West with 18 hundred cavalry made demonstrations
from Williamsburg yesterday morning Genl Kautz left
Suffolk this morning with three thousand cavalry
for the services indicated in conference with
the Lt General ---- The New York Flag
of truce boat was found lying at
the wharf with four hundred Rebel prisoners
which she had not had time to
deliver ---- she went the yesterday morning
---- We are landing the troops during
the night a hazardous service in face
of the enemy BF Butler 
Maj Gen

===== 7666 Page 8 | {}
notwithstanding the fire of the fleet had been opened upon them 
for five hours - in the meanwhile the surf had 
so risen as to render further landing nearly impracticable
---- after a thorough reconnoissance of the work finding it
utterly impracticable for a land assault & that at
least two brigades of Hoke's Div from before Richmond
had arrived there & that the rest was on
the road I with drew the forces & ordered
a reembarkation & had got on board
all of the troops with the exception of about three hundred
when the surf was so high as to prevent
Either getting on or off the shore ---- I
lay by until morning & took measures for their
relief as soon as the sea might go down ---- They
were under cover of the Gunboats & I have no 
doubt they are all safely off - our loss
when I left was but 12 wounded ten of
whom were by the shells of the Navy on our
picket line near the fort - I will be up
in the morning Signed copy of this to Prest. U. S. 
Benj F. Butler Maj Genl

===== 8602 Page 124 | {}
against Fort Anderson & the adjacent works on both sides
of Cape Fear now . yesterday while the guns maintained
a heavy fire upon Ft Anderson I pressed the Enemy
on both sides of the river & sent a force under
Genl Cox about sixteen miles around a swamp to turn
the Enemys right . This force made its way along 
a narrow defile between two swamps & completely turned the
Enemy's position . as soon as the movement became known to the Enemy
he abandoned his works & retreated toward Wilmington . We 
captured ten guns uninjured & a considerable amount of ammunition
. We have about fifty prisoners . The loss in
killed or wound is small on either sides . The
troops are pursuing the enemy & the gunboats are moving 
up the river . Fort Anderson & its collateral works
are very strong & rendered almost inaccessible by swamps .
a small force could have held them until their supplies
were exhausted . my information is that the rebels have
a line of defence behind Town creek where they propose
to make a stand if so it can probably be
only a short one . only four brigades of my 
troops have arrived from Wash & no transportation but I
will keep at work with what I have . I
am Genl very Respectfully your obt. serv't.
JM Schofield
Maj Genl

